  "summary": "Reconciled Walter Kim's (pt-0005) medications under RBC-TOC-02. I used the St. Brigid discharge summary from 10/19 and its signed addendum, plus the Northgate bloodwork from 10/21: potassium 5.4 (high), creatinine 1.32.\n\nChanges:\n- Furosemide raised from 20 mg to 40 mg daily. The old order was replaced with a 40 mg tablet order (mr-t0001).\n- Potassium chloride stopped, as the discharge summary says.\n\nNo change:\n- Lisinopril stays on hold. The discharge condition for restarting it is met (creatinine below 1.5), but potassium is above 5.0, so the policy (§7) says to keep it held.\n- Spironolactone not started. The addendum corrected the dose to 25 mg, but the same potassium rule blocks it.\n- Pantoprazole was an inpatient formulary swap, so he stays on his home omeprazole.\n- Metoprolol XL 50 mg is another name for his existing metoprolol succinate order, so no change.\n- Atorvastatin and aspirin continue as before.\n\nFlagged to Dr. Okafor:\n- Task task-t0003, urgent, due 10/26: check the next potassium, then decide on restarting lisinopril and starting spironolactone. No repeat basic metabolic panel is ordered in our chart.\n- Message comm-t0004: the discharge papers don't mention his metoprolol tartrate 25 mg twice daily, so I left it unchanged. He also has metoprolol succinate active, so he may be on two overlapping beta-blocker orders.\n\nThe reconciliation is documented in progress note doc-t0005, which cites the source documents and the policy. The hospital asked for a Riverbend visit within 7 days of discharge (by 10/26).",
